A GPU (Graphics Processing Unit) is a specialized electronic circuit originally designed to accelerate the rendering of images and video. Unlike a CPU (Central Processing Unit), which handles general-purpose computing tasks sequentially across a few powerful cores, a GPU features a massively parallel architecture with thousands of smaller cores designed to process multiple data streams simultaneously.
While they are widely known for powering high-end video games and graphical user interfaces, modern GPUs are heavily utilized for compute-intensive workloads outside of traditional graphics, including:
 * Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning: Accelerating the training and inference of deep learning models, natural language processing frameworks, and neural networks.
 * Video Production and Rendering: Speeding up rendering times for visual effects, video editors, and 3D modeling software.
 * Data Processing: Handling heavy matrix and vector math required for scientific research, simulations, and automated data processing pipelines.
Depending on your system setup, a GPU can either be integrated (built directly onto the same die as the CPU to save power) or discrete/dedicated (a separate, high-performance hardware card with its own dedicated VRAM).

